          "selector": "#bs-tile-select-confirm"
        },
        {
          "type": "clickSelector",
          "selector": "#bs-param-strip [data-field=\"spacing\"] .bsx-stepper-btn:first-child"
        },
        {
          "type": "dragTiles",
          "x1": 25,
          "z1": 25,
          "x2": 29,
          "z2": 29
        },
        {
          "type": "screenshot"
        },
        {
          "type": "clickSelector",
          "selector": "#bs-tile-select-confirm"
        }
      ],
      "role": "player",
      "expect": {
        "equals": {
          "orderedHoleCount": 9,
          "holeCount": 0
        }
      },
      "description": "depth corrected from 8 to 6 (Ground rule #15/Finding #42's class, the whole-suite audit before Phase 3): this step never clicks the grid tool's depth stepper (data-field=\"depth\"), so the real drag always produces the panel's own 6m default regardless of what's declared. This file's own expect blocks only assert hole/charge/sequence counts around the affected blast (depth-insensitive), so this is a metadata-only correction -- verified both modes still pass unchanged. #553: spacing tightened from 5m to 2m to keep this 9-hole grid's drilling time reasonable, now with two drillers/drill_rigs working it in parallel (a single driller cannot finish before the 120-tick worker-revolt countdown -- see the second `employee hire role:driller` step above)."
    },
    {
      "command": "tick 50",
      "description": "#553 follow-up: drain the ordered plan in bounded chunks rather than one large tick count -- each chunk is followed by `event choose 0` since a `tick` command stops dead the instant a timer event fires mid-block (events.ts), silently short of the requested count. With two drillers working in parallel, sandbox-measured (pure command-mode engine probe) landing all 9 holes around tick ~297-310 from a drill_plan at tick 102, comfortably inside the worker-revolt countdown's tick ~333 deadline -- the last (5th) chunk below is deliberately smaller than the others to land in that window with margin rather than overshoot into revolt.",
